  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 333 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nStrong mid/upper low centered near northeast Montana/northwest\nNorth Dakota and southern Saskatchewan will continue to slide \nnortheast through tonight. This system is responsible for the \nongoing rain and expected period of windy conditions tonight. \nThe evolution of the larger scale pattern over the next 7 days \nwill initially be complicated across the Northern Plains as \nstalled troughing to the west and weaker ridging in the central \nCONUS places us in a split- flow type set-up before the upstream\ntrough breaks loose. Trend should be for warmer temperatures \nthan the last 3 days, however due to the flatter nature of the \nridging, we should be mild rather than hot. As climate averages \nare in the lower 70s and dropping towards the upper 60s by the \nend of this month, this pattern with some locations in the mid \nto upper 70s is above average but not that unseasonable.\n\nRegarding precipitation chances beyond today; there is a low\nchance for a light shower/sprinkle Tuesday afternoon with \ncyclonic flow and CAA aloft lingering in our north (wetting rain\nnot expected). There is a weaker/progressive mid level \nshortwave Tuesday (main wave in the northern stream in Canada) \notherwise better chances for wetting rain do not arrive until \nFriday-Sunday as the upstream trough breaks loose and tracks \nover the Northern Plains. Evolution/details remain variable and \nat this time this should be a much weaker trough passage than \nwhat we are seeing today lowering the chance for widespread \nwetting rain. \n\n...Rain Showers Today...\n\nThe main area of rain is continuing to transition east over \nnorthwest MN with scattered showers and some areas of drizzle\ndue to lingering deeper BL saturation in southeast BL flow. The\nmain dry slot featuring well defined subsidence is already \nmoving across western ND towards eastern ND and organized shower\nactivity should exit our region west to east. Drizzle/lower \nlevel rain processes will be harder to pin down and may linger \nalong with light fog into the evening before clearing as winds \nstart to shift more to the southwest in the BL. In any case, dry\nconditions are favored by late evening. \n\n...Winds Tonight...\n\nA cold front already starting to push east over wester ND will\narrive later this evening and increased CAA 925MB will result \nin dry adiabatic mixed layer off the surface. While there may be\na shallow nocturnal inversion, pressure rises and strong CAA at\n925MB should help create at least a period of stronger winds \nfrom the west then northwest as the front passes. Winds near the\ntop of the mixed layer may be near 50kt over parts of the \nDevils Lake Basin, and this raises the potential for a few \nlocalized gusts 58mph+, though more frequent gusts with the \nperiod of stronger winds would be 45-50mph. Due to the direction\nof the winds and orientation of unidirectional flow through the\nmixed layer being westerly it is less favorable for stronger \ngusts east of the Red River Valley escarpment, which matches \nhigher resolution guidance in placement of advisory conditions \nmainly west of the valley in the 10pm-7am period. There is still\npotential for a few gusts 45-50 mph farther east or south than \nthe current advisory, however location/duration/coverage are \nmuch less certain due to the previously mentioned flow/wind \ndirection. It will be something to monitor in the event we have \nto expand the advisory based on trends this evening.",
